export const meta = {
  name: 'agents-md-workflow-review',
  description: 'Review AGENTS.md workflow and the three review sub-agent definitions for coherence and correctness',
  phases: [
    { title: 'Analyze', detail: 'five lenses over AGENTS.md + .claude/agents/*.md' },
    { title: 'Verify', detail: 'adversarially check each lens\u2019s findings against the files' },
    { title: 'Synthesize', detail: 'rank surviving findings into concrete edits' },
  ],
}

const REPO = '/home/scottdensmore/Developer/scottdensmore/super-star-trek'

const COMMON = `
You are reviewing the agent-workflow documentation of a C/CMake game repo at ${REPO}.

Files that matter:
- ${REPO}/AGENTS.md  (the shared cross-agent instruction file; CLAUDE.md just imports it)
- ${REPO}/.claude/agents/ui-review.md, verifier.md, code-review.md  (the three review sub-agent definitions)
- ${REPO}/.codex/config.toml, ${REPO}/.codex/hooks.json, ${REPO}/.cursor/hooks.json, ${REPO}/.github/ (other tools' wiring)
- ${REPO}/CMakeLists.txt, ${REPO}/tests/ (what the tests actually are)

READ the actual files. Quote file:line for every claim. Do not speculate about content you have not read.

Known-already-filed GitHub issues (do NOT report these as new findings; you may note if one is understated):
#125 verifier.md builds without -DSST_WERROR=ON, which is what CI uses
#110 No floor under "Answering a finding": the section can be gutted silently
#86 code-review's brief quotes sst.doc, and nothing tells it not to trust the quote
#85 The parts of a session's injected context go stale independently of each other
#84 ui-review's description points into AGENTS.md, and its reader has no fresh copy
#81 Nothing checks that the agent definitions are loadable or agree with AGENTS.md
#65 Nothing checks that AGENTS.md's workflow numbering stays consistent

Report ONLY findings that would change what a maintainer does: a rule that contradicts another rule, an instruction an agent cannot follow, a gap that lets a required pass be silently skipped, a sub-agent definition that does not implement what AGENTS.md says it does, or wiring that does not work. Style preferences and prose-quality opinions are out of scope unless they cause a real misreading.
`

const FINDING_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    findings: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string', description: 'one-line statement of the problem' },
          location: { type: 'string', description: 'file:line or file:section' },
          evidence: { type: 'string', description: 'quoted text from the files that shows it' },
          why_it_matters: { type: 'string', description: 'the concrete failure: what an agent does wrong because of this' },
          suggested_change: { type: 'string', description: 'concrete edit, quoting replacement wording where it is short' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocker', 'should-fix', 'nit'] },
        },
        required: ['title', 'location', 'evidence', 'why_it_matters', 'suggested_change', 'severity'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['findings'],
}

const LENSES = [
  {
    key: 'contract-drift',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

LENS: Does each of the three sub-agent definitions actually implement the job AGENTS.md assigns it?

Go step by step. For each of ui-review (step 6), verifier (step 7), code-review (step 8):
- What does AGENTS.md require of that pass — including the "Who runs which tests", "The review sub-agents" and "Answering a finding" sections?
- What does the definition file actually tell the agent to do?
- Where does the definition fall short, contradict, or exceed the mandate?

Pay specific attention to: what the verifier is told to run vs what tests actually exist in ${REPO}/tests and CMakeLists.txt (are test names/commands in verifier.md real?); whether ui-review's instructions cover the TUI/tmux and 72x24-80x24 rules AGENTS.md sets; whether code-review's "Review coverage" section matches what step 8 asks the author to disclose; whether any definition asks for something AGENTS.md forbids (e.g. reviewers editing files, reviewers filing issues).`,
  },
  {
    key: 'sequencing',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

LENS: Is the 12-step workflow logically sound as a state machine?

Trace a realistic change through steps 1-12 and look for: ordering that cannot be satisfied; rerun conditions that loop forever or never trigger; a pass whose precondition another step invalidates; ambiguity about WHO does something; steps that reference other steps by number incorrectly; the interaction of step 6's ui-review rerun rule, step 7's verifier rerun rule, step 8's code-review rerun rule and step 11's "do not repeat" rule — do they compose, or can they deadlock/contradict?

Also check: what happens when a ui-review fix arrives after the verifier ran? What happens when code-review requests a change at step 8 — which passes must rerun, and does the text actually say so? Is there any path where a commit lands with a pass that never saw the committed state?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'wiring',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

LENS: Does the machinery exist and work?

Check concretely:
- Do the three .claude/agents/*.md files have valid Claude Code frontmatter (name, description, tools) and would Claude Code load them? Do the declared 'tools' lists suffice for what each definition asks the agent to do? (e.g. can verifier run tmux/ctest with only Bash, Read, Grep, Glob? can ui-review build?)
- AGENTS.md says non-Claude tools must point at .claude/agents/*.md and carry only wiring. Read ${REPO}/.codex/config.toml, ${REPO}/.codex/hooks.json, ${REPO}/.cursor/hooks.json and anything under ${REPO}/.github/hooks and .github/workflows. Do they actually do that? Is the rule satisfiable for those tools, or is it aspirational text describing wiring nobody has built?
- Is there any automated check (CI, ctest, hook) that the workflow's own rules are followed? What breaks silently if a definition file is deleted or renamed?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'delegation-model',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

LENS: Is the division of labour between main agent and sub-agents correct and followable?

Focus on the "Who runs which tests" section and step 4. Questions to settle from the files:
- Is "the test that is the subject of the change" a criterion an agent can apply without guessing? Where is it genuinely ambiguous?
- The mutation-proof delegation: AGENTS.md says to use "a general-purpose sub-agent" on a copy outside the repository. Is that reachable/expressible in this harness? Is the guidance about copied build/ directories accurate given CMakeLists.txt? Check whether the claims about which scripts read the tree vs take a binary argument (help, tui, golden, instructions, score, tournament) are TRUE by reading ${REPO}/tests/*.sh and CMakeLists.txt.
- Does the context-size argument hold up — are the cited numbers (47 failures/1209 lines, 3000 bytes, four minutes per ctest configuration) checkable, and does anything contradict them?
- Is there work assigned to nobody, or assigned to two parties at once?`,
  },
  {
    key: 'gaps',
    prompt: `${COMMON}

LENS: What is missing that a repo with this workflow ought to have — and what in the doc is now false?

- Are there roles the workflow needs but has no sub-agent for? Argue both sides; a proposal to ADD a fourth sub-agent must say what it catches that the existing three provably do not, per their definition files.
- Conversely: is any of the three redundant, or does any overlap another enough to waste a pass?
- Are there statements in AGENTS.md that are stale or false against the current tree? Check specific factual claims: the tmux invocation, the piped plain-mode journey command (does it actually work against ./build/sst?), the ctest -R examples, file paths, the sst.doc line citations mentioned (sst.doc:436-439).
- Is the doc's length/structure itself a defect — is any rule likely to be missed because of where it sits? Only report this if you can point at a specific rule that a step's reader would not reach.`,
  },
]

phase('Analyze')

const VERDICT_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    kept: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string' },
          location: { type: 'string' },
          evidence: { type: 'string' },
          why_it_matters: { type: 'string' },
          suggested_change: { type: 'string' },
          severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocker', 'should-fix', 'nit'] },
          verifier_note: { type: 'string', description: 'what you confirmed, and any correction to the original claim' },
        },
        required: ['title', 'location', 'evidence', 'why_it_matters', 'suggested_change', 'severity', 'verifier_note'],
      },
    },
    refuted: {
      type: 'array',
      items: {
        type: 'object',
        properties: {
          title: { type: 'string' },
          reason: { type: 'string', description: 'the quoted text that refutes it' },
        },
        required: ['title', 'reason'],
      },
    },
  },
  required: ['kept', 'refuted'],
}

const results = await pipeline(
  LENSES,
  l => agent(l.prompt, { label: `find:${l.key}`, phase: 'Analyze', schema: FINDING_SCHEMA }),
  (found, lens) => {
    if (!found || !found.findings || !found.findings.length) return { kept: [], refuted: [] }
    return agent(`${COMMON}

You are an ADVERSARIAL VERIFIER. Another agent produced the findings below about this repo's agent workflow. Your default is to REFUTE. For each finding:

1. Open the cited file and read the surrounding text IN FULL — not just the quoted fragment.
2. Check whether the quote is accurate and whether the surrounding text already answers the objection. This documentation is dense and self-referential; many apparent gaps are covered three paragraphs later or in a different section ("Who runs which tests", "The review sub-agents", "Answering a finding").
3. Check whether it duplicates one of the already-filed issues listed above. If it does, refute it.
4. Kill anything that is a prose/style opinion, a restatement of a rule the author chose deliberately, or a claim you cannot confirm from the files.
5. For survivors, sharpen: correct the location, tighten the evidence, and make the suggested change concrete enough to apply.

Be strict. A finding that only "could be clearer" is refuted. Findings to judge:

${JSON.stringify(found.findings, null, 2)}`, { label: `verify:${lens.key}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA })
  }
)

const kept = results.filter(Boolean).flatMap(r => r.kept || [])
const refuted = results.filter(Boolean).flatMap(r => r.refuted || [])
log(`${kept.length} findings survived verification, ${refuted.length} refuted`)

phase('Synthesize')

const synthesis = await agent(`${COMMON}

You are writing the final review of this repository's agent workflow, for the repo owner. Below are findings that survived an adversarial verification pass, gathered by five independent lenses. They may overlap or duplicate each other.

Read ${REPO}/AGENTS.md and the three ${REPO}/.claude/agents/*.md files yourself so your synthesis is grounded, not just a merge of other agents' text.

Produce a markdown report with exactly these sections:

## Verdict
Two or three sentences: does the workflow hold together, do the three sub-agents make sense in it, and is anything actually broken?

## What works
Short. Only the structural choices that are genuinely well-made, with a reason.

## Changes to make
The surviving findings, deduplicated and merged, ordered blocker -> should-fix -> nit. For each: a bolded one-line title, the file:line, one sentence on the concrete failure it causes, and the specific edit to make (quote replacement wording where it is under ~40 words). Skip anything that is only a style preference.

## Sub-agent roster
Whether the three roles are the right three: any redundancy, any missing role, and your recommendation with a reason. Say plainly if the answer is "the three are right as they are".

## Already filed
Findings that map onto existing open issues (#125, #110, #86, #85, #84, #81, #65), one line each, so the owner does not file duplicates.

Be direct and concise. No preamble, no flattery. If a finding is weak, leave it out rather than padding the list.

FINDINGS:
${JSON.stringify(kept, null, 2)}

REFUTED (do not resurrect these, but they show what was considered):
${JSON.stringify(refuted.map(r => r.title), null, 2)}`, { label: 'synthesize', phase: 'Synthesize' })

return { report: synthesis, keptCount: kept.length, refutedCount: refuted.length }

